**:<b>Intelligent Agents</b>
An agent is an entity that perceives its environment through sensors and acts upon it using actuators.
The complete record of everything an agent has perceived is called a percept sequence.
The agent's behavior is defined by a function that maps percept sequences to actions.;

***:<b>Rational Agents</b>
A rational agent is one that, for any given percept sequence, selects the action expected to maximize its performance measure.
    - Rationality aims to maximize expected performance, which is different from perfection, where actual performance is maximized.
    - A rational agent may need to perform actions to gather information and explore unknown environments.
    - Agents that learn over time and adapt their behavior are called autonomous agents.;
*** Task Environment (PEAS)
****:<b>Performance Measure</b>
The criterion that defines the agent's success.;
**** Environment
***** Fully vs. Partially Observable: Whether the sensors provide complete access to the environment’s state.
***** Deterministic vs. Stochastic: Whether the next state is completely determined by the current state and action.
***** Episodic vs. Sequential: Whether the agent’s experiences are divided into independent episodes (perception → action).
***** Static vs. Dynamic: Whether the environment can change while the agent is deliberating.
***** Discrete vs. Continuous: Whether the number of percepts and actions is finite and well-defined.
***** Single-Agent vs. Multi-Agent: Whether there is only one agent or multiple agents that may be cooperative or competitive.
****:<b>Actuators</b>
The components that allow the agent to act on the environment (e.g., motors that move a robot).;
****:<b>Sensors</b>
Devices that detect the environment (e.g., cameras, infrared sensors, microphones).;
***:<b>Types of Agents</b>
****:<b>Simple Reflex Agents</b>
React only to the current percept using condition–action rules (“if–then”). Work best in fully observable environments.;
****:<b>Model-Based Reflex Agents</b>
Maintain an internal state that tracks aspects of the world not directly observable, allowing them to handle partially observable environments.;
****:<b>Goal-Based Agents</b>
Choose actions based on specific goals, enabling more flexible and adaptive planning.;
****:<b>Utility-Based Agents</b>
Extend goal-based agents by using a utility function to evaluate and select the action that produces the highest expected satisfaction or performance.;

